@prefix atom: <http://lv2plug.in/ns/ext/atom#> .
@prefix lv2: <http://lv2plug.in/ns/lv2core#> .
@prefix pset: <http://lv2plug.in/ns/ext/presets#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix state: <http://lv2plug.in/ns/ext/state#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<>
	a pset:Preset ;
	lv2:appliesTo <http://open-music-kontrollers.ch/lv2/notes#notes> ;
	state:state [
		<http://open-music-kontrollers.ch/lv2/notes#text> """Many reverbs/delays/IR's, when following NAM, will create a horible burst of noise when the pedalboard is first loaded.

The cause is NaN (not a number) output from NAM.  A reverb/delay with feedback can recycle this bad output creating a nasty digital cacophony.

A few reverbs seem to be NaN-safe including: DISTRHO DIE Reverb, and the X42 """ ;
		<http://open-music-kontrollers.ch/lv2/notes#fontHeight> "20"^^xsd:int
	] .

